DECISION
AR-H124 is POSITIVE
for:
MEMORY-PRICED SPARSE INTERFACE ECOLOGY

WITH:
a RETAIN-EVERYTHING / STALE-BRIDGE LIMIT.

Supported synthetically:

- retaining every bridge becomes expensive as memory price rises;
- a fixed bridge-count cap is too crude;
- value-per-memory exact retention strongly improves net value;
- shared translator modules plus exact residuals perform best in the tested ecology;
- old bridge knowledge can retain recurrence value;
- bridge storage and current execution authority should remain separate;
- context shift can invalidate previously correct direct shortcuts.

Not demonstrated:

- learned translator-family discovery;
- semantic family misclassification;
- online bridge-memory compaction;
- multi-level translator grammar;
- graph-wide cache coherence;
- real runtime memory cost.

NEXT HIGH-VALUE MOVE
AR-H125 — TRANSLATOR-FAMILY FALSE SHARING / INTERFACE GRAMMAR COLLISION AUDIT

H124 assumes:
interface families
really share:
